this is a production of Cornell  University all right thanks Sarah as she  said my name is Jenna Hershberger I'm a  second year grad student in my course  lab and today I'll be talking about one  of several projects that I'm working on  and this one's called an Android  application for near-infrared  spectroscopy based you know typing of  Kesava quality traits so this project is  part of a larger Fino Apps project  that's funded by NSF  and this project is led by Mike Gore as  well as Jesse Poland who is here a few  weeks ago so the main problem that the  pheno apps project is looking to address  is this one typically when reading  programs take notes it's by hand on  paper you get bookshelves that look like  this this is an actual photo from ITA of  their breeding data also lovingly known  as old-school cassava base so you know  apps integrate data collection with the  actual new cassava base out of BTI and  that's one way that we are increasing  the efficiency with which breeding  programs can take phenotypic data but  beyond this Fino apps also have the  possibility of collecting data that's  not possible by hand such as spectral  data and image data so one of the other  important parts of the pheno apps  project is workshops so deployment of  the apps and this these are pictures  from a workshop that we held at iit a in  march and so we train breeders on how to  use the apps and ask for feedback on the  apps that are being used and then  potential apps that could be developed  in the future so we asked the breeders  what traits are you working on that  could use a more efficient tool and we  got a lot of responses so one of them  that we hadn't really put too much  thought into before is dry matter  content in cassava so dry matter content  is really important to cassava breeders  to farmers and to  consumers cassava roots are the  harvested part of the plant they're  mostly starch in the dry matter is what  is actually cared about so the dry  matter is a large component of yield  it's used as a proxy for eating quality  and it's really important for farmers  which factors into then variety adoption  as they want to grow crops that are  actually going to be consumed by Zoomers  and can be used for processing for  eating things like that so the problem  though is cassava dry matter can be  difficult to measure so there are two  main ways that it's normally measured  through the specific gravity method and  the oven method so on the right we have  the specific gravity method as measured  last year in one of Hollis fields Paul  is another lab member of mine so this  method takes you take the roots still in  the field take a subset of them from  your plot weigh them in air and then in  water and use a simple equation to  translate that into dry matter content  the other method is the oven method  which you take your samples back to the  lab you peel your roots you shred them  you measure them before and after they  go through the drying process and that's  how you get dry matter content the there  is though a third way that people have  started using and this is nears or  near-infrared spectroscopy so this is  being used as it's there's a benchtop  spectrometer that's being used to see  out there other organizations as well  that have jumped on board and it's been  proven highly accurate fast and also  yeah it's just very useful so the  problem though is you still have to take  all your samples back to the lab process  them and then put them through the  spectrometer so there's another version  of this where instead of being in the  lab you have a handheld unit and so this  is something that you go out of Jean  Luc's lab is exploring and he has shown  that also that method can have high  accuracy but the problem with these is  the cost is prohibitive so before I go  into that I'm going to talk a little bit  about what yours is  so Neer's is this region of the  electromagnetic spectrum from 700 to  2500 nanometers and the reflectance as  measured by a spectrometer can be  indicative of the chemical composition  of whatever you're measuring so it  changes the reflectance patterns based  on the carbon hydrogen nitrogen hydrogen  and oxygen hydrogen bonds total carbon  carbon and nitrogen so this chart shows  some of the properties that can be  measured by nears and then this is a  screenshot from one of the apps we're  working on that shows some variation  between different routes for these  traits so because the cost is  prohibitive for the NIR spectra  spectroscopy but it's a promising  technology we've been looking into lower  cost options to use nearest in the field  so we found this device it's called scio  it has a smaller range than what's been  used in the past to measure dry matter  content in cassava and so in order to  move forward with this technology I've  been working with Hugo to subset his  data from his handheld years that has  high accuracy and see if this range is  still accurate in predicting dry matter  using his developed models so with that  we've gone we have reduced the accuracy  from 96.7% with holdout cross-validation  to 93.3 but 93.3 is still pretty high  for a tool that is a 200 times reduction  in price so this each unit is about 300  dollars instead of tens of thousands of  dollars so we're looking at something  that is actually feasible for breeders  to be using in their programs and could  be really useful in taking dry matter  data quickly in the field without having  to transport samples and also accurately  so in order to then move forward with  this project Paul  I set up a small pilot experiment  upstairs in the lab here using roots  from Wegmans and that you can see in the  picture on the left here we're just  practicing the protocol for scanning the  roots  the device has a small plastic shield by  the actual spectrometer and that you can  put flesh with the root slice which the  roots are already sliced a phenotype for  a different diseases so handy there you  can just use the slices that are already  cut and scan them so we've worked on the  protocol and now have moved into a pilot  field trial so Paula is currently in  Uganda harvesting her G wast trial for  softness and the roots are already being  phenotyped for specific gravity as well  as these disease traits that require the  slicing of the root so we've hired an  extra team member for her to be able to  scan the roots as part of the  phenotyping process and these same roots  are then being taken first the specific  gravity measurements and we also have  some of them going for the oven method  just to round it all out so this harvest  is taking place starting this week  these pictures are actually from last  year but this is the idea of what will  be happening so and then after we get  the data from this pilot trial we will  then be making some prediction models  I'm looking into using partially squares  regression which will reduce the  dimensionality of the spectral data and  account for the multi collinearity  between wavelengths and then we'll be  doing some cross-validation and then  hopefully some auditioning additional  phenotyping for model improvement with  more more measurements of dry matter and  scans prepared to be able to predict  better and ultimately all this will be  packaged into an app for Android that  will be able to join the rest of the  pheno apps in this suite of apps that  are all free on Google Play for breeders  to use and  with that I would like to thank Paula  and ago especially for their help on my  project as well as my committee Mike  Rebecca and Tim the rest of the gore lab  the pheno ops team and so thanks and  I'll take questions  yeah so with those we really doubt  there's a lot of variation with those  routes we did measure them using a  drying oven and there wasn't a ton of  variation but some of the routes were  actually statistically different from  each other in dry matter so we are  waiting on a research license to be able  to look at the actual spectra to start  our actual model development so we don't  know right now how that's going to look  but we'll see that's why we actually  need a pilot experiment to larger scale  be able to tell  yeah  so yeah so for this tool it has a little  cover on it that you can take off and  then turn the device over put it back in  the cover and you press a button and it  will calibrate for you  so in our protocol we're doing that  between plots it's probably a little bit  overkill but we'd rather have that then  not have it correctly calibrated okay  all right if there are no more questions  let's thank Jenna one more time this has  been a production of Cornell University  on the web at cornell edu
 

